      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="ComboBox" name="cmb_scope">
        <property name="minimumSize">
         <size>
          <width>180</width>
          <height>19</height>
         </size>
        </property>
        <property name="maximumSize">
         <size>
          <width>999</width>
          <height>19</height>
         </size>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Scope — which objects Bake Lightmaps acts on.

Selected (default) — the selected meshes.
Visible — every visible mesh in the scene.
Scene — every visible mesh in the scene (Arnold renders no hidden object, so a hidden one is skipped and named).

Objects in the Exclude set are skipped whatever the scope.

The light button beside the field takes the scene's environment (HDRI skydome) in or out of the bake.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="frame">
         <bool>false</bool>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <layout class="QHBoxLayout" name="exclude_layout">
        <property name="spacing">
         <number>1</number>
        </property>
        <item>
         <widget class="QLabel" name="lbl_exclude">
          <property name="minimumSize">
           <size>
            <width>0</width>
            <height>19</height>
           </size>
          </property>
          <property name="maximumSize">
           <size>
            <width>16777215</width>
            <height>19</height>
           </size>
          </property>
          <property name="toolTip">
           <string>Exclude — objects that get no lightmap of their own but still light the bake: they cast shadows and bounce light onto everything that does bake.

Stored in the scene (the lightmapBaker_exclude set), so every bake of this scene skips them — from this panel or a script — and a re-bake leaves a map they already have alone.</string>
          </property>
          <property name="text">
           <string>Exclude:</string>
          </property>
          <property name="caption" stdset="0">
           <bool>true</bool>
          </property>
         </widget>
        </item>
        <item>
         <widget class="QPushButton" name="set_exclusions">
          <property name="sizePolicy">
           <sizepolicy hsizetype="Expanding" vsizetype="Fixed">
            <horstretch>1</horstretch>
            <verstretch>0</verstretch>
           </sizepolicy>
          </property>
          <property name="minimumSize">
           <size>
            <width>0</width>
            <height>19</height>
           </size>
          </property>
          <property name="maximumSize">
           <size>
            <width>16777215</width>
            <height>19</height>
           </size>
          </property>
          <property name="toolTip">
           <string>Make the current selection the Exclude set (replacing it). A selected group excludes every mesh under it; with nothing selected, the set is cleared.</string>
          </property>
          <property name="text">
           <string>Set From Selection</string>
          </property>
         </widget>
        </item>
       </layout>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="ComboBox" name="cmb002">
        <property name="minimumSize">
         <size>
          <width>180</width>
          <height>19</height>
         </size>
        </property>
        <property name="maximumSize">
         <size>
          <width>999</width>
          <height>19</height>
         </size>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Packing — how the baked maps are laid out.

Atlas by Material (default) — objects sharing a primary material are consolidated into one shared EXR (Resolution becomes the shared atlas size), and each object gets an area-weighted atlas rect bound at engine time (Unity's lightmapScaleOffset / glTF KHR_texture_transform) — UVs are never edited, and instances are fully supported: every copy keeps its own rect and its own lighting. Fewer textures, no naming collisions, more efficient texel use for many small objects. The layout is planned up front, so each object bakes at 4x the size it will occupy (never above a full map) instead of a full map per object: the downscale into the atlas is what averages Arnold's sampling noise.

Per-Object — one lightmap texture per object at the full Resolution. Best per-object detail; right for hero assets or small selections.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="frame">
         <bool>false</bool>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="ComboBox" name="cmb_device">
        <property name="minimumSize">
         <size>
          <width>180</width>
          <height>19</height>
         </size>
        </property>
        <property name="maximumSize">
         <size>
          <width>999</width>
          <height>19</height>
         </size>
        </property>
        <property name="toolTip">
         <string>Which processor Arnold renders the bake on. A machine setting, not a quality one — it is never stored in a preset.

Auto — the GPU wherever Arnold has one, else the CPU. At the same quality the GPU is the fast one: measured on a production floor, 3.4s on the GPU against 24.2s on the CPU for the same ray budget.
GPU — force it, with no fallback.
CPU — force it. Use this if a GPU bake ever disagrees with a CPU one: Arnold's GPU renderer does not support every shader feature.</string>
        </property>
        <property name="frame">
         <bool>false</bool>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="QGroupBox" name="quality_group">
        <property name="sizePolicy">
         <sizepolicy hsizetype="Preferred" vsizetype="Fixed">
          <horstretch>0</horstretch>
          <verstretch>0</verstretch>
         </sizepolicy>
        </property>
        <property name="title">
         <string>Quality</string>
        </property>
        <layout class="QVBoxLayout" name="quality_layout">
         <property name="spacing">
          <number>1</number>
         </property>
         <property name="leftMargin">
          <number>0</number>
         </property>
         <property name="topMargin">
